The API with the lifecycle discipline, verified:

```js
// BIND with a scope — one abort controller per component/page
function mountSearch(container) {
  const scope = new AbortController();          // the whole mount's teardown

  container.addEventListener('input', onInput, { signal: scope.signal });
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize, {
    signal: scope.signal,
    passive: true,                               // won't preventDefault → smooth
  });
  document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey, {
    signal: scope.signal,
    capture: true,                               // catch before children
  });
  const timer = setInterval(autoSave, 30_000);

  return () => {                                // the ONE dispose function
    scope.abort();                               // removes EVERY signal listener
    clearInterval(timer);                        // timers are NOT covered
  };
}

// REMOVAL RULE — exact reference + type + capture match
el.removeEventListener('click', onClick);        // works: same function ref
el.removeEventListener('click', () => {});      // NEVER works: new function
```

The options (each one answers a real problem): once — one-shot UI (first-scroll banner reveal); passive — the browser doesn't wait for your handler on scroll-blocking events (wheel, touchstart, and on modern browsers scroll listeners default passive on window/document — an explicit preventDefault there logs a warning); capture — see the event before target-phase handlers (interception, focus/blur which don't bubble); signal — the modern answer to 'remove 12 listeners on unmount' — one abort() call.

The leak anatomy (why listeners leak and what they take with them): a listener on a LONG-LIVED target (window, document, a persistent parent) captures its handler's closure — variables, DOM nodes, component state — and keeps them unreachable-for-GC as long as the TARGET lives. Remove the component but not the listener, and the listener keeps its whole world alive: the 'detached DOM' grows in memory with every mount/unmount. The audit pattern: Performance panel → 'Detached nodes' / heap snapshot, listener breakpoints, and getEventListeners(el) in the DevTools console (the hidden inspection API).
